A||||19||FLASHY||Anyone own, used to own, or know much about a Nissan 300 zx?||||1||
Z||000000||FLASHY||05-10-2001||03:43 PM||||Hey everyone! Sorry if this is a repetitive topic. I did a search and couldn't find much help so now I'm asking...<P>My wife is looking into getting a sporty car and she's taken a liking to the '90-'91 Nissan 300 zx. Does anyone have any input on these cars? Are they very reliable, expensive to maintain, known for any serious problems? I read that Slammed Fiero used to work for Nissan, Spyder has or had one, 87ECO5 posted a 300 zx forum address (which I'm going to check out), and 1FST2M6 had a '93. Any advise from them or anybody else would be greatly appreciated. Thanks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Trust me, I've tried talking her into a fiero but she wants to have something else. I guess she's too Fiero'd-out from all my car talk. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||beer_stud_21||05-10-2001||04:44 PM||||I was lookin at the current car and driver the other day- There was even a Fiero owner in the letters section asking for them to correct there previous mention that the new Z is mid engined, it is not, but they previously said it was because the engine is behind the front wheels.  Anyways, in that same reader section there is a letter from a guy who said he loved the older 300Z, he said it was more fun than his current porshe boxter.  I dunno about reliability, but i'm sure the TT 300Z isnt cheap to repair. Personally I think that body style is awsome....  I love it.  Dare I say cleaner than a fiero...<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||05-10-2001||06:31 PM||||If the engine is behind the front wheels and in front of the back wheels, then the car is mid engined. I don't see what the big fuss is over the body style either, I guess it's just a matter of personal preferance.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||05-10-2001||08:57 PM||||I have a good friend that has a 90. Its the baseline model, no turbos. Its pretty fast, looks pretty nice, hes had it for like 6 years now and he likes it for everyday trans. Parts prices seem to be on a par with higher line Hondas, Acura. Hes got about 150K on it. Only real maint hes had to do over the ordinary was replace water pump, a real ***** . It is in sad need of new paint. I never drove one, but I hear the turbo models are very fast, but thats just heresay.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shaft||05-10-2001||09:30 PM||||I've heard the 90 automatics had problems, but other than that they're really good cars. I drive a 90 Nissan Maxima everyday and it runs just as well as the day we bought it (in 90) As long as the car has had regular maintenance I wouldn't doubt the cars reliability<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||05-10-2001||10:37 PM|||| I rode in a friends 91 non turbo 300Z and was very impressed. Handled better than my Stealth ever hopes to(avoid Stealth/3000GT like the plague!!!) and was decent speed wise.<P> Mmmm....Supra.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FLASHY||05-11-2001||12:09 AM||||Cool, thanks for the info so far guys! So the automatics had some problems around then huh? That's not good to hear since that's what she wants. I don't think we'd consider the turbo just because she won't really need it and of course the extra costs that come with it if something goes wrong.<P>Shaft: Do you know if it was only the '90 model automatics that had problems?<P>Any more information?||||reg||10
Z||000007||ChadMan||05-11-2001||09:11 AM||||The fastest car I have ever driven was a 1993 300ZX twin-turbo.  (Though I drove an automatic C5 Corvette that might have been faster had we turned the traction-control off...)<P>They are great cars.  The only problem I know of is that the fuel injectors on the previous generation 300's (wedge-shaped, 83-90?) tended to leak and Nissan recalled all of them to fix it (Maximas too).  Not sure if the last-gen 300's had that problem.||||reg||14
